<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Aegis.Application</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Aegis.Domain\Aegis.Domain.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Orchestration primitives and abstractions. Note what is absent: no SQL Server provider, no
    Redis client, no HTTP client, no ASP.NET Core. Application declares the ports it needs
    (IAegisDbContext, ICacheService, ICurrentUser, ITenantContext) and Infrastructure supplies
    the adapters. That inversion is what lets a handler be unit-tested without a database.

    On Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore below: this is a deliberate, documented compromise, not an
    oversight. IAegisDbContext exposes DbSet<T>, because the purist alternative (IQueryable<T>)
    costs Include, AsNoTracking, ExecuteUpdateAsync and every async terminal operator — recovering
    those means hand-rolling an async query abstraction larger than the thing it hides. What
    matters is preserved: this is the EF Core *abstraction* package, with no provider, no
    connection string and no migrations. See IAegisDbContext for the full reasoning.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="MediatR" />
    <PackageReference Include="FluentValidation" />
    <PackageReference Include="FluentValidation.DependencyInjectionExtensions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Mapster" />
    <PackageReference Include="Mapster.DependencyInjection" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
